\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Serotonin induces a DOWN state under anesthesia. (a) An example spike raster plot of 10s of spiking activity recorded on two Neuropixel probes simultaneously. Recorded brain regions are indicated on the right of the raster at their approximate depths. The time of the optogenetic 5-HT stimulation is indicated by the blue bar (25 Hz, 10 ms pulses). (b) Peri-stimulus time histogram of an example neuron from the frontal cortex. The bottom shows the spike raster in which each row is a repetition of 1s 25 Hz stimulation. The top shows the mean spiking rate binned in 50 ms bins with a 25 ms Gaussian smoothing kernel. *** \emph  {p} $<$ 0.001, ZETA-test. (c) Percentage of significantly modulated neurons (ZETA-test, \emph  {p} $<$ 0.05) per brain region, calculated per recording session. Black dots are sessions, grey bars means over sessions. (d) Modulation index per neuron, negative values indicate the neuron is inhibited by 5-HT and positive values indicate excitation. Each grey dot is a single neuron, vertical red bars are the mean over neurons per brain region. (e) An example peri-stimulus raster plots of spike times (black ticks, each row is a neuron), aligned to the onset of 5-HT stimulation (blue vertical bar). The orange line indicates the posterior probability of a DOWN state as determined by a two-state HMM. On the left an example from the frontal cortex is shown and on the right an example from the amygdala. (f) A peri-stimulus heatmap over trials of when the HMM determined there most likely was a DOWN state (orange). The black line is the mean posterior probability of a DOWN state over trials per time bin. (g) A peri-stimulus plot of the change in DOWN state probability compared to baseline (-1 to 0) averaged over mice (n = 5, mean $\pm $ sem). Plotted separately, indicated by color, are different stimulation frequencies. (h) Peri-stimulus plots for all recorded high-level brain regions of the change in DOWN state probability over baseline for 25 Hz serotonin stimulation.}}{3}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ephys}{{2}{3}{Serotonin induces a DOWN state under anesthesia. (a) An example spike raster plot of 10s of spiking activity recorded on two Neuropixel probes simultaneously. Recorded brain regions are indicated on the right of the raster at their approximate depths. The time of the optogenetic 5-HT stimulation is indicated by the blue bar (25 Hz, 10 ms pulses). (b) Peri-stimulus time histogram of an example neuron from the frontal cortex. The bottom shows the spike raster in which each row is a repetition of 1s 25 Hz stimulation. The top shows the mean spiking rate binned in 50 ms bins with a 25 ms Gaussian smoothing kernel. *** \emph {p} $<$ 0.001, ZETA-test. (c) Percentage of significantly modulated neurons (ZETA-test, \emph {p} $<$ 0.05) per brain region, calculated per recording session. Black dots are sessions, grey bars means over sessions. (d) Modulation index per neuron, negative values indicate the neuron is inhibited by 5-HT and positive values indicate excitation. Each grey dot is a single neuron, vertical red bars are the mean over neurons per brain region. (e) An example peri-stimulus raster plots of spike times (black ticks, each row is a neuron), aligned to the onset of 5-HT stimulation (blue vertical bar). The orange line indicates the posterior probability of a DOWN state as determined by a two-state HMM. On the left an example from the frontal cortex is shown and on the right an example from the amygdala. (f) A peri-stimulus heatmap over trials of when the HMM determined there most likely was a DOWN state (orange). The black line is the mean posterior probability of a DOWN state over trials per time bin. (g) A peri-stimulus plot of the change in DOWN state probability compared to baseline (-1 to 0) averaged over mice (n = 5, mean $\pm $ sem). Plotted separately, indicated by color, are different stimulation frequencies. (h) Peri-stimulus plots for all recorded high-level brain regions of the change in DOWN state probability over baseline for 25 Hz serotonin stimulation}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\citation{brunelCommunicatedPaulTiesinga2006}
